<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of the Sabis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/57-bce">57 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Battle of the Sambre · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallic-wars">Gallic Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of the Sabis was fought in 57 BCE on the river Sabis, usually identified with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sambre">Sambre</a>, in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. The Nervii, with the Atrebates and Viromandui, surprised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>'s legions while they were making camp. After hard fighting, in which Caesar himself joined the front line, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> destroyed the Nervian army, and much of Belgic Gaul submitted.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-sabis.jpg" alt="Battle of the Sabis" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Boerkevitz · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SamberMerbesleCh%C3%A2teau.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the winter of 58–57 BCE Caesar heard that the Belgae were forming a union under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galba-suessiones">Galba</a>, king of the Suessiones, out of fear of Roman interference. He raised two new legions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisalpine-gaul">Cisalpine Gaul</a>, won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/remi">Remi</a> to his side and, after defending their oppidum of Bibrax and winning an action at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aisne">Aisne</a>, saw the union break up for lack of provisions. Tribe after tribe surrendered, but the Nervii, Atrebates, Aduatuci and Viromandui refused. The Nervii, who allowed no luxuries into their land, were said to be the most hostile to Roman rule. Caesar had eight legions, perhaps 30,000–45,000 fighting men; the Remi reckoned the Nervii alone at 50,000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Hidden in woods across the shallow river, the Belgae waited for the Roman baggage train to appear, then rushed across and charged the legions as they fortified their camp, giving them no time to form up. On the left Legions X and IX drove the Atrebates into the river, and in the centre VIII and XI pushed back the Viromandui. A column of Nervii under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boduognatus">Boduognatus</a> poured through the gap this opened, attacking the camp and surrounding Legions XII and VII on the right. Camp followers fled, and the Treveri cavalry rode home to report a disaster.</p><p>Caesar took a shield, went to the front line of the hard-pressed Legion XII, called his centurions by name and had the ranks open out. The two legions on the right were brought back to back, the new Legions XIII and XIV arrived with the baggage, and Labienus, having taken the Belgic camp, sent Legion X back across the river against the Nervii's rear. The Nervii fought on from the bodies of their dead until the last few fled.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Nervian elders told Caesar their council had fallen from 600 to three and their 60,000 fighting men to barely 500; Caesar says he spared them. He gives no Roman losses, and his account is almost the only source. The Aduatuci turned home and were later defeated, some 53,000 of them sold into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. The Nervii fought again in the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambiorix">Ambiorix</a> in 54 BCE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of the Sabis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They joined the Belgic league in 57 BCE, promising fifteen thousand men, and fought beside the Nervii at the <b>Sabis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/atrebates">Atrebates</a></li><li><blockquote>At the <b>Sabis</b> (probably the Sambre) in 57 BCE they surprised Caesar's camp and were crushed only after a desperate battle.</blockquote><a href="/state/nervii">Nervii</a></li><li><blockquote>They fought beside the Nervii and Atrebates at the <b>Battle of the Sabis</b> in 57 BCE and were defeated.</blockquote><a href="/state/viromandui">Viromandui</a></li></ul></section>
